  "rules": [
    "When recent history shows a sharp price spike (e.g., >5% increase) followed by high sentiment scores (0.20+), the model systematically underestimates the immediate upside and fails to capture the momentum, resulting in a persistent negative bias for the first 3-5 days of the forecast horizon.",
    "The model exhibits significant mean reversion lag; it predicts a smooth upward trend after volatility spikes, whereas true outcomes show high volatility with frequent reversals (e.g., dropping from ~89 to ~77 within 10 days), causing the model to overestimate prices in the middle-to-late forecast horizon.",
    "Sentiment history of 0.50 or higher at the start of a spike period correlates with an 'overshoot' error where the model's linear projection misses the initial acceleration, requiring a downward correction of 3-6% on the first few forecast days to align with actual volatility.",
    "In high-volatility regimes (history range >15 points), the model's assumption of monotonic growth is invalid; forecasts should be adjusted to include a 'volatility buffer' that accounts for the probability of price drops occurring within the first 2 weeks of the forecast window."
  ]
}
